course: language
lesson: 24
title: Borrowed words from far away: Hindi, Arabic, Chinese, Ukrainian and more
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Words travel with people, ships, trade and food; when English speakers met people from India, the Arab world, China, Japan and Ukraine, they came home with new words.
explain: Priya's grandma in Leicester smiles: "Pyjamas, shampoo and bungalow are Hindi words, beta!" ("beta" is a loving word for a child in Hindi and Urdu), and she is right.
explain: Your breakfast may be full of travellers: "coffee" and "sugar" came from Arabic, "tea" from a Chinese language, and "ketchup" probably started as a Chinese fish sauce.
explain: Many words changed on the way, passing through several languages and sometimes changing meaning, so a word's story can be like a map of a long journey.
explain: When a word's past is not certain, honest experts say "probably" or "likely", and so should we.
fact: "Pyjamas" (leg garment), "shampoo" (first meaning press or massage) and "bungalow" (a house in the Bengal style) came from Hindi and Urdu during British rule in India.
fact: "Jungle" came from Hindi "jangal", wild land, and "loot" from Hindi "lūṭ", stolen goods.
fact: "Algebra" comes from Arabic "al-jabr", putting broken parts together, from a maths book by the scholar al-Khwarizmi in Baghdad in the 800s; his name gave us "algorithm".
fact: "Giraffe", "coffee" (qahwa) and "sugar" (sukkar) came from Arabic, often through other languages like Turkish, Italian or French.
fact: The idea of zero came from India; Arab mathematicians passed it to Europe, and its name came from Arabic "ṣifr", empty, which also gave "cipher".
fact: Many experts think "ketchup" comes from Hokkien, a Chinese language, "kê-tsiap", a fish sauce; this is likely but still argued about, and it became a tomato sauce in America in the 1800s.
fact: "Kowtow" comes from Chinese for "knock head", a deep bow touching the forehead to the ground; in English it now means being too eager to obey.
fact: "Tea" came from Hokkien "te", carried by sea; "chai" (Ukrainian "чай") came from Mandarin and Cantonese "chá", mostly over land; both go back to Chinese.
fact: "Borscht", the beetroot soup closely linked to Ukraine, reached English through Yiddish from the Slavic word "борщ"; in 2022 UNESCO listed Ukrainian borscht cooking as heritage needing urgent protection.
fact: "Cossack" came into English from Ukrainian "козак" (kozak) and Russian "kazak"; it probably goes back to a Turkic word for a free person; Cossacks were free warrior communities on the Ukrainian steppe, a huge grassy plain.
fact: From Sanskrit, the ancient language of India, came "guru" (teacher), "pundit" (learned person) and "avatar" (a god coming to earth in a body, now also your character in a game).
fact: From Japanese came "tsunami" (harbour wave), "emoji" (picture + character) and "karaoke" (empty orchestra).
exercise: Priya's grandma says "pyjamas" is a Hindi word. Is she right? | Yes, it means leg garment | No, it is French | No, it is Latin | No, it was made up by a shop
exercise: What did "shampoo" first mean in Hindi? | Press or massage | Soap made from flowers | Bubbles in the bath | Wash your face clean
exercise: What is a "bungalow" named after? | A house in the Bengal style | A kind of drum played in the jungle | A bungee jump | A low wall
exercise: What did Hindi "jangal" mean? | Wild land | A rainforest with monkeys | A loud noise | A tiger's home
exercise: Sara doesn't believe "algebra" is Arabic. What did "al-jabr" mean? | Putting broken parts together | Counting the stars in the night sky | A big number | A clever person
exercise: Whose name gave us the word "algorithm"? | Al-Khwarizmi's | Aladdin's | Archimedes' | Alfred the Great's
exercise: Which three words came from Arabic? | Giraffe, coffee and sugar | Pyjamas, shampoo and jungle | Tsunami, emoji and karaoke | Guru, pundit and avatar
exercise: What did Arabic "ṣifr" mean? | Empty | Circle | Nothing to eat | Ten of something
exercise: Mei's grandma says ketchup was Chinese first. What do many experts think? | It probably started as a Chinese fish sauce | It was always made of tomatoes, right from the start | It was invented by an American chef in a diner | It came from French "ketch"
exercise: What does "kowtow" mean in English today? | Being too eager to obey | Milking a cow | Shouting loudly | Running away
exercise: Leah says "tea" and her Ukrainian friend says "чай". What is true? | Both words go back to Chinese | They are completely unrelated words | "Чай" comes from English "tea" | "Tea" comes from old Ukrainian
exercise: How did the word "borscht" reach English? | Through Yiddish from the Slavic word "борщ" | Through French cooking books written for rich families in the 1700s | Straight from Latin | From an American TV cookery show
exercise: Olena says "Cossack" comes from Ukrainian "козак". Who were the Cossacks? | Free warrior communities on the Ukrainian steppe | Ancient Roman soldiers who guarded the city walls | Sailors from Japan | Monks in English churches
exercise: Which language gave English "guru", "pundit" and "avatar"? | Sanskrit | Classical Arabic | Old Japanese | Ukrainian
exercise: What does "karaoke" mean in Japanese? | Empty orchestra | Loud singing | Happy party | Microphone
exercise: What does "tsunami" mean in Japanese? | Harbour wave | Big storm | Earthquake | Deep sea
test: Josh's mate goes "emoji is obviously from emotion lol, no cap." ("No cap" means no lie.) What's true? | It's Japanese for picture + character; the link with "emotion" is a coincidence | He's right, it's short for "emotion icon", made up by an American phone company | It's from "emu" | It's Latin for face
test: Your cousin says "zero was invented in Europe, obvs." What's true? | The idea came from India and its name came through Arabic | She's right, it came from Rome, along with the Roman numerals | It came from Japan | It came from Ukraine in 1900
test: Adam's gamer bro says "avatar was made up by game programmers, innit." What's fair? | It's an old Sanskrit word that found a new life in games | He's right, it's from the 1990s | It's from Arabic "ava" | It's a brand name
test: Your mate says "jungle always meant rainforest, end of." What's true? | Hindi "jangal" meant wild land, and the meaning changed in English | He's right, it has always meant a hot rainforest full of monkeys and vines | It meant "lion" | It meant "tree" in Latin
test: What would happen if English had never met Arabic? | It might lack words like "coffee", "sugar" and "algebra" | It would have no numbers at all, not even one, two and three | It would have no food words | Nothing would change in English
test: Mykola's mate says "borscht? that's just a Russian word, mate." What's a fair answer? | Borscht is a Ukrainian dish, and its name reached English through Yiddish | He's right, it's only Russian, and the soup has nothing to do with Ukraine at all | It's a French word | It's a Japanese word
test: Your dad says "these word stories are all certain facts." What should you say about ketchup? | Its Chinese origin is likely, but experts still argue about it | Dad's right, it's 100 percent proven by old Chinese recipe books | It definitely came from Latin | Nobody has any idea at all
test: A game shows a pirate in 1600 squirting tomato ketchup. What's off? | Ketchup became a tomato sauce in America in the 1800s | Pirates hated sauce | Ketchup was invented in 2000 | Nothing, ketchup was always tomato, even in the 1600s
test: Puzzle: one word for the drink sailed to Europe on ships, the other came over land. Which English word came by sea? | Tea, from Hokkien "te" | Chai, from Mandarin "chá" | Coffee, from Arabic "qahwa" | Sugar, from Arabic "sukkar"
test: Your uncle says "kowtow is about cows bowing". What's true? | It comes from Chinese for "knock head", a deep bow | He's right, it's about cows | It's a Scottish word for a cowshed on a windy hill farm | It's from Arabic for "milk"
exam: Identify the origin of the English word "algorithm". | The name of the ninth-century Baghdad scholar al-Khwarizmi | The Greek term for a rhythmic sequence of numbers employed in music | The Latin term for calculation | A Japanese word for a method
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the words "tea" and "chai"? | Both derive ultimately from Chinese, by maritime and overland routes respectively | They are entirely unrelated in origin, the former being Chinese and the latter of Persian derivation | "Chai" derives from the English "tea" | Both derive from Arabic
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the word "ketchup". | It is likely, though disputed, that it derives from a Hokkien term for a fish sauce | It is proven beyond doubt to be of French origin | It has always denoted a tomato sauce | It was invented in the twentieth century
exam: State the language from which English obtained the words "pyjamas", "shampoo" and "bungalow". | Hindi and Urdu | Japanese | Ukrainian | Arabic
